course: dictionary
lesson: 7665
title: Who is this message for?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Propaganda is information, often false or one-sided, spread to make people believe something or support a group, leader or idea. You can use the clues around the word to work out what it means, just as you would with any new word.
explain: «Propaganda is a message made to push people towards one view, often by a government or a group, and it picks only the facts that help.» The clue is «picks only the facts that help». Propaganda shows one side of the story and leaves the rest out.
explain: «Propaganda often uses fear, anger, music and colours instead of reasons, and may call a whole group "enemies".» When a poster makes you feel scared or proud but gives no proof, stop and ask who made it and why.
fact: Propaganda is information spread to make people believe something or support a group, leader or idea.
fact: It is often false or one-sided, and it can use posters, films, slogans and social media.
fact: Propaganda plays on feelings like fear or pride instead of giving reasons.
fact: Useful phrases are «war propaganda», «a propaganda poster» and «spread propaganda».
fact: A good question to ask about any message is: who made this, and why?
exercise: «Propaganda often uses fear, anger, music and colours instead of reasons.» What does this tell you propaganda tries to do? | Ask you to check the facts yourself | Give the full facts with proof from both sides | Show a calm scene of nature | *Make people feel something rather than think it through
exercise: In a history lesson, you see «a propaganda poster» from wartime. What is a propaganda poster? | A painting bought for a museum | A notice about the school timetable | A map showing where the roads are | *A poster that spreads a message to make people believe or support something
exercise: «Propaganda is a message made to push people towards one view... and it picks only the facts that help.» Find and copy one word that shows it is one-sided. | *picks | government | message | group
test: What is propaganda? | A kind of fancy dance | A school subject about maps | A type of weather | *Information, often one-sided, spread to make people believe something
test: Why might a message call a whole group «enemies»? | *To make people fear and dislike them without good reasons | To prove the group is kind and helpful | To ask for help with homework | To show where a group lives on a map
exam: A poster shows only the good facts about its leader and makes you feel proud, with no proof. What is this? | A dictionary entry | *Propaganda | A fact sheet | A weather report
